CONTEXT="Jam is bored and asks the innkeeper about somewhere interesting nearby. She directs him east to the lord's castle;he recognizes the Japanese term and cheerfully decides to visit. When he returns,Lil demands to know where he has been. He excuses himself with the heavy object,identifies it boastfully as a genuine made-in-Japan figurehead,and claims the locals gave it to him for teaching them its use. Lil accepts only if he did not steal it and tells him to prepare to leave. A separate castle cutaway shows a frightened Guard reporting the missing shachihoko and a letter in foreign writing to the irritated morning-time Daimyo,who explicitly orders the thief found. Four actorless FE letter parts reveal Jam's confidently mistaken ship/castle explanation,his guess at incomplete transmission,his unilateral taking of one supposed spare,his one-per-ship reminder,and the full Jam Jack Ludwyan signature. Preserve the tension among his gift claim,the letter and the theft accusation. No particular castle,language,king,emperor,marriage,kinship,arrest,punishment,successful escape or legitimate gift is established. Speaker fields support Lil Argot,Jam Ludwyan versus full Jam Jack Ludwyan,Guard,Daimyo and Innkeeper;unused Japan metadata is not a spoken biography."
